[Page:Overview]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(Overview)

[PageHeader:Overview]
Overview

[Overview]
<div class="paragraph">
<b>Mugsy</b> (Melbourne House, 1984) is a "comic strip" strategy game.
You are Mugsy, boss of a 1920s protection racket, and your sidekick Louey reports to you each year in speech bubbles
over full-screen pictures. You decide how many clients to buy or sell, how much to spend on artillery and ammo, how
many clients to squeeze, how much to hand to the cops as a kickback, and whether to pay off rival mobs or your own
disgruntled hoods. The game is meant to last 7 to 11 years (chosen at random, but see the #LINK(Bugs#years)(bug about the game rarely ending on time)) unless you are rubbed out first, and ends with a
percentage score and a rating.
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<b>Loading.</b> The tape holds a BASIC loader (CLEAR 24575, then LOAD "s" CODE 16384 for the loading screen and
LOAD "mugsy" CODE for 40,960 bytes at #R24576-65535) and starts the game with PRINT USR 24576 (#R24576). There is no
copy protection; the TAP and TZX images in this project have identical code and screen blocks.
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<b>Economics.</b> The yearly model, what each of your answers really does and the best strategy are on the
#LINK(Economics)(economics page). In short: squeeze everybody, buy clients, spend about 1.5 grand per hood on ammo,
pay a large (but not total) kickback, keep nothing in the safe, and pay a small handout (about 0.1 grand per hood) that
gives Mugsy the full 8 hit points for the shootout.
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<b>Structure.</b> Almost all of the game logic is written not in Z80 code but in a compact bytecode, interpreted by
the routine at #R30092 (see the #LINK(VM)(VM reference)). The script for one year is the block at #R24652. The VM has
84 signed 32-bit fixed-point variables (two decimal places; see the #LINK(Variables)(variables page)), nine flag
bytes at #R33888, and op 14 calls native Z80 routines for input (#R27790, #R27753), the hush-money scene (#R28297),
the end-of-game rating (#R26740), the "A year goes by" animation (#R33994) and the hit-man shootout (#R28358).
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<b>Text.</b> Louey's lines (#R49774) are compressed. The 32 commonest words are single bytes (96-127, via #R49710) and
the other words are two-byte references into a dictionary of 225 words (#R48640). Control codes insert numbers and
money values taken from the VM stream after the SAY instruction, pick random alternatives, or repeat earlier text (#R29458).
The rendered text is word-wrapped into an oval speech bubble (#R28757) in a six-pixel-high proportional font
(#R27087), while the status lines use the ROM font (#R27724).
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<b>Pictures.</b> The bubble backgrounds are vector pictures drawn by #R31247 from command lists (lines, flood fills,
bitmap blocks and attribute runs), chosen at random from two tables of layouts (#R33909). The two full-screen pictures
used in the animations (the Partridge Club and the street) are in a more compact bit-stream format drawn by #R32126.
All nine pictures are shown on the #LINK(Pictures)(pictures page). The picture engine is a descendant of the one in The Hobbit; see the #LINK(Hobbit)(Hobbit page).
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<b>Animations and arcade sequence.</b> At the end of each year one of two IM2-driven animations plays (#R33994): the
hoods passing through the Partridge Club, or the getaway car. Nothing in the game decides which: it is a random choice, and because the random routine is uneven the club comes up about 60% of the time and the car about 40% (see the #LINK(Facts#yeargoesby)(trivia page)). If your hoods put out a contract on you, the hit man from
Detroit arrives and you play an arcade shootout in a restaurant (#R42112), controlled with Q/Z (up/down), I/P
(left/right) and B/N/M (fire).
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<b>Memory map.</b>
#TABLE(default,centre,:w)
{ =h Address | =h Contents }
{ #R24576-24651 | Start-up and main loop }
{ #R24652-26739 | VM script for one year }
{ #R26740-28403 | Keyboard, input and font printing }
{ #R27087-27723 | Proportional font }
{ #R28404-30013 | Status lines, speech bubbles and the text renderer }
{ #R30014-31217 | Random numbers, the VM interpreter and fixed-point arithmetic }
{ #R31218-32830 | Picture drawing (both formats) }
{ #R32832-33550 | Theme tune }
{ #R33552-33936 | VM variables, flags and bubble layouts }
{ #R33937-37100 | Initialisation and the end-of-year animations }
{ #R37101-41996 | Animation graphics }
{ #R41997-48638 | The hit-man shootout (code, data and graphics) }
{ #R48640-53352 | Dictionary, common words and messages }
{ #R53353-53792 | Leftover assembler source, the interrupt jump and the IM2 vector table }
{ #R53793-65535 | Pictures }
TABLE#
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<b>How this disassembly was made.</b> The code was separated from the data by running the game in SkoolKit's Z80
simulator with scripted keypresses for many games (recording every executed address), then following every branch
statically from those addresses. The VM scripts were traced from the nine places that start the interpreter, and the
messages were decoded with a Python reimplementation of the text renderer. About 95% of the code instructions have comments written
by hand (the shootout, the animations and the picture routines were also checked by running them in the simulator); the
rest carry comments generated from a table of known variables and routines, so they say what the instruction does in
game terms where that is known and literally otherwise.
The generated mugsy.asm also assembles, with an unmodified copy of sjasmplus 1.24.1 (<code>sjasmplus --raw=out.bin mugsy.asm</code>), to exactly the same 40,960 bytes as the tape, in addition to SkoolKit's own skool2bin check.
</div>

[Page:VM]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(VM)

[PageHeader:VM]
The VM

[VM]
<div class="paragraph">
The interpreter (#R30092) keeps its program counter at #R30090 and its accumulator in BCDE, with a second operand in
BC'DE' (called alt in the table below). "v" operands are one-byte variable numbers; "addr" operands are two-byte addresses. Comparisons jump to the
address when the condition holds. The flags returned by each instruction survive into the next, so JZ after CALL tests
the result of the native routine.
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
#TABLE(default,centre,:w,:w,:w)
{ =h Op | =h Mnemonic | =h Operands | =h Action }
{ 0 | END | | Return from the interpreter }
{ 1 | SAY | addr, flags, [extra] | Show the message at addr in a speech bubble (#R28549). Any number or money placeholders in the message take one variable number each from the bytes that follow; sub-message placeholders take an address. }
{ 2 | SETMSG | addr | Set the default sub-message (used by a sub-message placeholder whose address is 0) }
{ 3 | IFEQ | v1, v2, addr | Jump if v1 = v2 }
{ 4 | IFNE | v1, v2, addr | Jump if v1 &lt;&gt; v2 }
{ 5 | IFLE | v1, v2, addr | Jump if v1 &lt;= v2 }
{ 6 | IFLT | v1, v2, addr | Jump if v1 &lt; v2 }
{ 7 | IFGT | v1, v2, addr | Jump if v1 &gt; v2 }
{ 8 | IFGE | v1, v2, addr | Jump if v1 &gt;= v2 }
{ 9 | IFPEEKEQ | addr1, n, addr2 | Jump to addr2 if PEEK addr1 = n }
{ 10 | IFPEEKNE | addr1, n, addr2 | Jump to addr2 if PEEK addr1 &lt;&gt; n }
{ 11 | POKE | n, addr | POKE addr, n }
{ 12 | JZ | addr | Jump if the zero flag is set }
{ 13 | GOTO | addr | Jump }
{ 14 | CALL | addr | Call a Z80 routine }
{ 15 | STORE | v | v = acc }
{ 16 / 17 / 18 | ADD | - / v / v1,v2 | acc = acc+alt / acc+v / v1+v2 }
{ 19 / 20 / 21 | SUB | - / v / v1,v2 | acc = acc-alt / acc-v / v1-v2 }
{ 22 / 23 / 24 | MUL | - / v / v1,v2 | acc = acc*alt / acc*v / v1*v2 (fixed point) }
{ 25 / 26 | DIV | v / v1,v2 | acc = acc/v / v1/v2 (0 if dividing by 0) }
{ 27 | INT | | acc = whole part of acc }
{ 28 | LOAD | v | acc = v }
{ 29 / 30 | RND | - / v | acc = a random number from 0 to acc (or v), worked out on the low byte only, so RND v71 (v71 = 1.00) gives 0.00 to 1.00 (see the #LINK(Facts#random)(trivia entry on random numbers)) }
{ 31 | NEG | | acc = -acc }
{ 32 | HALF | v | acc = v/2 }
{ 33 / 34 | SQRT | - / v | acc = square root of acc (or v) }
TABLE#
</div>

[Page:Variables]
PageContent=#INCLUDE(Variables)

[PageHeader:Variables]
VM variables

[Variables]
<div class="paragraph">
All values are stored multiplied by 100. Money is counted in thousands of dollars ("grand").
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
#TABLE(default,centre,centre,:w,:w)
{ =h Var | =h Address | =h Name | =h Meaning }
{ 0 | #R33552 | StartClients | Number of clients at the start of the game (400) }
{ 1 | #R33556 | Squeezed | Number of clients squeezed this year }
{ 2 | #R33560 | Raided | Amount (in grand) taken by the cops in a raid }
{ 3 | #R33564 | Hoods | Number of hoods in the mob (shown on the status line) }
{ 4 | #R33568 | Killed | Number of hoods rubbed out last year }
{ 5 | #R33572 | Price | Average protection payment per client }
{ 6 | #R33576 | Year | Current year (starts at 1) }
{ 7 | #R33580 | Dough | Dough in the safe, in thousands of dollars (shown on the status line) }
{ 8 | #R33584 | Clients | Number of clients (shown on the status line as CUSTOMERS) }
